//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 62 - THE SHORT LEG'S LOCATION FIX, CANDIDATE 1 OF 2: MULTI-TOUCH RESISTANCE.
//
// STALE-PROMPT CHECK: the stored scheduled prompt describes a board state (Attack 37, "earned a
// filter stack") more than twenty attacks stale. Attack 41 closed Attack 37, Attack 46 is the long
// champion (filters exhausted, HARD LESSON 49), Attack 60 built the short mirror honestly (25%-equity
// sizing fix, HARD LESSON 42/43) and it failed on H1 (PF 0.593), Attack 61 tried an EMA200 regime gate
// on top of it and failed HARDER (PF 0.235, trades 67->15). THE DOCS OVERRIDE THE PROMPT, again, per
// the prompt's own instruction. This cycle does not restart Attack 37's filter stack (long since done)
// and does not propose a thirteenth long-only mechanism; it takes up Attack 61's own queue item 1,
// now ranked first: fix the short's LOCATION with a multi-touch resistance requirement, the other
// named candidate from Attack 60's queue that Attack 61 (regime gate) did not touch.
//
// ==================== THE CLAIM UNDER TEST ====================
// Attack 60/61's resistance is the single most-recent 20-bar high -- a level that may have been set by
// one spike and never actually defended. A level tested only once carries no information about whether
// sellers will show up there again. A level that price has already failed to break TWICE is a level the
// market has demonstrated it respects, which is a different (and better-motivated) claim than "the
// trend is bearish" (Attack 61's gate, which made things worse). This is a STRUCTURE claim about the
// level itself, not a regime claim about the market.
//
// ==================== CONSTRUCTION ====================
// Everything from Attack 60 is UNCHANGED: same 20-bar resistance/support definition, same tap
// tolerance, same RR floor (3.5), same R floor (0.8% by exclusion), same max hold (192 bars), same
// 25%-equity declared-deviation sizing fix (HARD LESSON 42/43). The ONLY addition is one new AND term:
// the tapped resistance level must have been tested at least MINTOUCHES times (default 2, i.e. at
// least one prior test before today's tap) within a longer lookback window (default 100 bars). No
// arrays, no user-defined functions -- the count uses a plain for-loop accumulator over prior bars,
// which is native Pine control flow, not a UDF.
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg (SHORT ONLY, per HARD LESSON 31 scoping -- not blended with Attack 46's long):
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) - unchanged from Attack 60, EXCLUSION via rBig, never clamped.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) - unchanged, the tap bar's own high.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) - SHORT ONLY, a new candidate, not a patch to Attack 60's record.
//   BINDING (E17) - multiTouch is a pure AND term added to Attack 60's goShort; it can only remove
//        entries, never add them, so it necessarily binds if touchCount is ever below the floor for
//        any historical tap. Trade count is read FIRST, before the ratio, same as every level-target
//        build in this lab.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) - multiTouch (a property of the LEVEL's prior history) is independent of rBig
//        (absolute stop distance) and rrOk (reward geometry at THIS tap) -- none of the three can stand
//        in for either of the others.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8) - res is [1]-shifted (known before the current bar); the touch count
//        loop reads high[lookback+1] through high[lookback+touchLookback] -- strictly OLDER than the
//        20-bar window that DEFINES res -- against the same already-shifted res used for tapped/held.
//        A FIRST DRAFT of this loop searched high[1]..high[touchLookback] and was caught before running
//        H2: that range overlaps the very 20-bar window whose maximum IS res, so the bar that set the
//        level always counted as its own "prior touch" and the term was tautologically true on every
//        setup. Confirmed by a corrected-timeframe H1 landing byte-identical to Attack 60 (PF
//        0.59338308, 67 trades, to the cent) before the fix -- an instance of HARD LESSON 10 (measure a
//        term's own effect before trusting the conjunction). Fixed to search bars OLDER than the level's
//        own defining window, which is what "a PRIOR test of this level" has to mean.
//   OCCUPANCY (LESSONS 24/28/29) - pyramiding=1, unchanged.
//   CASCADE / MARGIN ARTIFACT (HARD LESSON 42/43) - the 25%-equity fix carries over unchanged from
//        Attack 60; VERIFY with get_trades that loser exit distances stay consistent (near the tap
//        bar's own high) before trusting the win rate, exactly as Attack 60 required.
//   SAMPLE-FLOOR RISK, NAMED BEFORE THE RUN (HARD LESSON 45) - Attack 60 ran 67 trades on H1. A
//        filter requiring a SECOND test of the same level within 100 bars is a materially rarer
//        condition than "any 20-bar high" and could plausibly cut trade count under the ~30-trade
//        floor (LESSON 12) or under the workable 60-350 band on its own; if H1 lands under ~30 trades
//        the result is a DIRECTION, not a verdict, regardless of what PF reads.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY. No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale.
//
// OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17):
//   * H1 PF >= 1.0 AND trades >= 30 AND loss distances consistent -> the location fix worked where the
//     regime gate did not; compare directly against Attack 60's 0.593/67 and Attack 61's 0.235/15, then
//     run H2 per the mandate's kill-rule structure.
//   * H1 PF >= 1.0 BUT trades < 30 -> a DIRECTION only (LESSON 12), not promotable, H2 still run to
//     characterise it but the verdict stays "unprovable sample", the same fate as Attack 34.
//   * H1 PF < 1.0 -> DISCARDED by the kill rule. No filters, no rescue, no H2. Multi-touch would then be
//     the SECOND location-fix candidate to fail (after the regime gate), and the short leg's queue would
//     move to "neither named candidate worked" rather than staying open on an untested guess.
//   * Trade count collapses far below Attack 60's 67 (e.g. under ~20) even before looking at PF -> the
//     sample-floor risk named above landed, and that alone is reportable independent of the PF sign.
strategy("BTC Attack62 short multi-touch resistance", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

lookback      = input.int(20,     "Level lookback, bars -- sets BOTH the resistance tapped and the low targeted")
tapTol        = input.float(0.10, "Tap tolerance, % -- how close to resistance counts as a touch")
minRRv        = input.float(3.5,  "Minimum reward:risk the GEOMETRY must offer, else skip -- mirrors Attack 46/60")
minRpct       = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
maxBars       = input.int(192,    "Max bars in trade")
eqFrac        = input.float(0.25, "DECLARED DEVIATION: fraction of equity per position -- HARD LESSON 42/43")
touchLookback = input.int(100,    "Window to search for PRIOR tests of the resistance level")
minTouches    = input.int(2,      "Minimum total tests of the level (including today's tap) required to fire")

sup = ta.lowest(low,   lookback)[1]
res = ta.highest(high, lookback)[1]

tapped  = not na(res) and high  >= res * (1 - tapTol / 100)
held    = not na(res) and close < res
setupOn = tapped and held

touchCount = 0
for i = lookback + 1 to lookback + touchLookback
    if not na(res) and high[i] >= res * (1 - tapTol / 100)
        touchCount := touchCount + 1
multiTouch = touchCount >= (minTouches - 1)

rShort = high - close
reward = na(sup) ? na : close - sup
minR   = close * minRpct / 100
rBig   = rShort >= minR
rrOk   = not na(reward) and rShort > 0 and reward / rShort >= minRRv

goShort = setupOn and rBig and rrOk and multiTouch

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

flat   = strategy.position_size == 0
qtyUse = strategy.equity * eqFrac / close

if flat and goShort and qtyUse > 0
    strategy.entry("S", strategy.short, qty = qtyUse)
    slPx   := high
    tpPx   := sup
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size < 0
    strategy.exit("SX", from_entry="S", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goShort ? 1 : 0, title="ShortSignal")
plot(res, title="Resistance")
plot(sup, title="TargetLevel")
plot(touchCount, title="TouchCount")
